From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] shell32: a share's folder is drawn at once, and once

David: a share's folder "draws the icons somewhat slowly at first and then
it draws them quickly after that, always doing a redraw of the icons".
On the lab network (a Linux Samba server, 3 ms round trip) File Explorer
drew a folder of 2000 files row by row, then drew everything again:

- Each folder's icon looked for a desktop.ini in that folder: a listing of
  the folder over the network, one row at a time as the list was drawn.
  On a share a folder is now looked into only when the server marks it
  read-only or system -- the rule Windows follows for customized folders
  (the server's attributes come from 0822). Wine's own disks keep no such
  attributes and are looked into as before.
- The view drew itself as soon as it was made, in the default Details
  layout, before File Explorer gave it the folder's own layout (Large
  icons, a grouping); then it was drawn again. It is now drawn with the
  rest of the window, once.
- A file whose icon is its own (a program's, an .ico, a shortcut's) was
  read on the window's thread while the list was drawn: on a share,
  several round trips a file with the window standing still. Up to a tenth
  of a second in each half second still goes on such icons in place, so a
  folder on a fast network is drawn once with its icons; past that, as
  Windows' File Explorer does on a slow share, the icon is read on the
  view's picture thread while the item shows a plain one.
- The icon cache held its lock while it read a file, so a thread reading
  a share's program icon stopped every other thread wanting any icon --
  the window drawing its list. The file is now read without the lock; an
  icon two threads read at once is added once.

Test: test/smbview-gate.sh with test/smbview-probe.c and test/smbshim.c
(mutants SG_MUTANT_VIEW_PAINTS_EARLY and SG_MUTANT_SYNC_REMOTE_ICONS in
shlview.c, SG_MUTANT_REMOTE_DESKTOP_INI in shlfolder.c,
SG_MUTANT_ICON_LOAD_LOCKED in iconcache.c).
